Hi,<p>

I have a problem with passing a class method. Let me give an example:

<code>

package A;

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    return bless {}, $class;
}

sub _exe {
   my ($self,$a) = shift;
   $self->$a->(); # probably wrong !!

}



package B;

use base 'A';

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    return bless {}, $class;
}

sub _what {
   my ($self,$y) = shift;
   ## HERE I would like to pass _print() with $y (let it be a string) 
   ## to package A, method _exe() so that it executes
   ##  _print($y) in B.... Is this possible??

}


sub _print {
   my ($self,$x) = shift;
   print "$x\n";

}

</code>

Question in code (there are probably typos but I just need an example so I can study it).  <p> I could just do :


<code>


package B;

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    return bless {}, $class;
}

sub _what {
   my ($self,$y) = shift;
   $self->_print($y);

}


sub _print {
   my ($self,$x) = shift;
   print "$x\n";

}
</code>


But this is not what I am after. How to do this and what is the most elegant way to share methods between classes if they are not in a classical parent child relationship? (note that B inherits from A but now i need to pass a method and a variable for that method from B to A to be executed by A) <p>
<p>
Thank you !
